            <ArticleTitle>[Aspirin and other nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs hypersensitivity--mechanisms, diagnostics and treatment].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>366-77</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Aspirin hypersensitivity syndrome includes several symptoms from the respiratory tract, skin and digestive system triggered by ingestion of aspirin or other nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Asthmatic attacks precipitated by aspirin or other nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs occur in about 10% of all asthmatic patients. In subjects with aspirin hypersensitivity disruption of synthesis of prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) and overproduction of cysteinyl leukotrienes (Cys-LT) seem to be crucial in the pathogenesis of bronchial symptoms. Double blind, placebo controlled challenges are regarded as a gold standard in the diagnosis of aspirin hypersensitivity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
